6.2 The actual Iris rule, from the decompiled artifact already in this repo

    _analysis/astra-sc34/NoiseTexture.txt - first line: Compiled from "NoiseTexture.java"
    class net.irisshaders.iris.targets.backed.NoiseTexture

      :99-130  private java.nio.ByteBuffer generateNoise()
                 :101-108  iconst_3; width; imul; height; imul; newarray byte
                           -> byte[3 * width * height]        *** 3 BYTES PER PIXEL ***
                 :110-113  new java/util/Random; dup; lconst_0; invokespecial <init>(J)
                           -> new java.util.Random(0L)
                 :115-117  aload_2; aload_1; invokevirtual java/util/Random.nextBytes([B)V
                           -> the entire buffer is filled by ONE nextBytes() call
      :69-97   void resize(int glId, int width, int height)
                 :84-94    texImage2D(target=3553, level=0, internalformat=32849, width, height,
                           border=0, format=6407, type=5121, buffer)
                           32849 = GL_RGB8, 6407 = GL_RGB, 5121 = GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE
                 :22-23    _pixelStore(3317 = GL_UNPACK_ALIGNMENT, 1)
                 :16-35    texParameteri: MIN 10241 = GL_LINEAR, MAG 10240 = GL_LINEAR,
                           WRAP_S 10242 = GL_REPEAT, WRAP_T 10243 = GL_REPEAT
                 :77-84    MAX_LEVEL 33085 -> 0; 33082/33083 -> 0; 34049 -> 0.0f

    So Iris 1.11.2 produces an in-memory GL_RGB8 ByteBuffer with 3 bytes per pixel, uploaded
    directly to a GL texture. It never encodes a PNG, and it emits no alpha channel.

    Naming note: the ledger cites "NativeImageBackedNoiseTexture", but the decompiled class
    available in this repo is net.irisshaders.iris.targets.backed.NoiseTexture. The name in
    the ledger does not match the artifact it points at.

6.3 Reproduction attempts against the actual file (all in memory)

    File facts: 64x64, bit depth 8, color type 6 (RGBA), alpha == 255 for all 4096 pixels,
    each channel spans 0..255 with std ~74 (uniform-looking).

    PIL decode (authoritative - see 6.4), first 6 pixels RGB:
      [32,180,96] [12,69,193] [1,88,112] [143,178,170] [23,90,177] [8,150,195]

    Java LCG re-implemented exactly:
      seed = (s ^ 0x5DEECE66D) & (2^48 - 1)
      next(bits) = ((s = (s * 0x5DEECE66D + 0xB) & (2^48 - 1)) >>> (48 - bits))
      nextBytes(n) = repeated nextInt(), consumed 4 bytes little-endian

    Random(0).nextBytes(3*W*H = 12288), row-major, first bytes:
      [96,180,32] [187,56,81] [217,212,122] ...
    Random(0).nextBytes(4*W*H = 16384) sliced as [:3] / [1:], per-pixel nextInt() low-3 /
    high-3 bytes, and three next(8) calls per pixel: all tested.

    RESULT: pixel-exact hits = NONE, over the full cross product of
      6 base patterns x per-pixel byte reversal {no,yes} x x-flip {no,yes} x y-flip {no,yes}.
    Only pixel 0 matches, and only under R/B reversal (predicted [96,180,32] vs actual
    [32,180,96]); pixel 1 already diverges (predicted [187,56,81] vs actual [12,69,193]).

    Substring search: the first 64 bytes of the actual RGB data are NOT present anywhere in
    a 4,000,000-byte Random(0).nextBytes() stream (find() == -1), nor is the per-pixel
    reversed variant, nor the first 16 bytes. Stride-4 extraction matches only 30-61 of 12288
    positions, i.e. chance level (~12288/256 = 48).

    Other seed-0 RNGs also fail (first 12 bytes):
      actual                        : [32, 180, 96, 12, 69, 193, 1, 88, 112, 143, 178, 170]
